\begin{proof}
Let $S_{\Omega}$ denote the group of all bijections from $\Omega$ to itself, under composition. For each $n\in\Omega$, define $f(n)\in S_{\Omega}$ by
\[
f(n)=\begin{cases}
\operatorname{id}_{\Omega},&n=1,\\
(1\ n),&n>1,
\end{cases}
\]
where $(1\ n)$ exchanges $1$ and $n$ and fixes every other element of $\Omega$. These permutations are distinct: if $m,n>1$ and $m\ne n$, then $(1\ m)(m) = 1$ while $(1\ n)(m)=m$; and the identity differs from every $(1\ n)$ with $n>1$. Thus $f:\Omega\to S_{\Omega}$ is injective. Since $\Omega$ is infinite, $S_{\Omega}$ contains infinitely many elements. By definition it is a group under composition, so $S_{\Omega}$ is an infinite group.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $S_{\Omega}$ be the set of all bijections $\Omega\to\Omega$. It is a group under composition: the identity permutation is the identity element, composition of bijections is a bijection, and every bijection has an inverse.

For each $n\in\Omega$ with $n\ge2$, let $\tau_n\in S_{\Omega}$ be defined by
\[
\tau_n(k)=\begin{cases}
n,&k=1,\\
1,&k=n,\\
k,&k\notin\{1,n\}.
\end{cases}
\]
This is the transposition $(1\ n)$. If $m\ne n$ and $m,n\ge2$, then $\tau_m(1)=m\ne n=\tau_n(1)$, so the permutations are distinct. Hence $\{\tau_n:n\ge2\}$ is infinite and is contained in $S_{\Omega}$. Therefore $S_{\Omega}$ is infinite, and thus it is an infinite group.
\end{proof}
